<!-- 3. PREMISE -->
<section class="lsv2-premise" id="lsv2-premise">
  <div class="lsv2-premise-head lsv2-reveal">
    <div class="lsv2-eyebrow">The premise</div>
    <h2 class="lsv2-h-section">A habit isn't only chemical. <span class="lsv2-italic">It's also a ritual.</span></h2>
    <p class="lsv2-lede">
      Decades of cessation research show the behavioral and sensory layers of a habit drive cravings <em>independently</em> of any active drug. Lio is built to satisfy that layer.
    </p>

    <div class="lsv2-premise-thesis">
      <p>"Sniffing either a pleasant or unpleasant odor reduced reported urge to smoke relative to the control odor."</p>
      <cite>— Sayette &amp; Parrott, 1999 · Exp. Clin. Psychopharmacol. <button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="3">3</button></cite>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lsv2-premise-points">

    <div class="lsv2-premise-point lsv2-reveal">
      <div class="lsv2-premise-point-num"><strong>01</strong>Behavioral</div>
      <div>
        <h3>The ritual is roughly half the habit.</h3>
        <p>A 1994 randomized study at Duke gave 48 abstinent smokers a black-pepper vapor inhaler, a mint inhaler, or an empty cartridge. The black-pepper group reported <em>significantly reduced</em> craving and withdrawal versus control — without any nicotine being delivered. <button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="1">1</button></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="lsv2-premise-point lsv2-reveal">
      <div class="lsv2-premise-point-num"><strong>02</strong>Olfactory</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Pleasant scents reduce peak urge.</h3>
        <p>A 2019 follow-up replication (n = 232) at Pittsburgh and Brown confirmed that a pleasant olfactory cue, sniffed at peak craving, produces a measurable drop in self-reported urge — sustained for several minutes. <button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="2">2</button></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="lsv2-premise-point lsv2-reveal">
      <div class="lsv2-premise-point-num"><strong>03</strong>Substitution</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Sensorimotor replacement is itself a treatment.</h3>
        <p>The UK NHS has trialed de-nicotinised cigarettes specifically because the act, the breath, and the throat sensation matter <em>independently</em> of nicotine. Lio applies the same principle without combustion or any active drug. <button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="4">4</button></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="lsv2-premise-point lsv2-reveal">
      <div class="lsv2-premise-point-num"><strong>04</strong>Mechanism</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Aromatherapy shows a small but consistent effect.</h3>
        <p>A 2013 randomized study in <em>JACM</em> and a 2022 controlled pilot (n = 58) both reported reduced nicotine craving following short inhalation of black-pepper or angelica essential oil. Effect sizes are modest. The effect is real. <button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="5">5</button><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="6">6</button></p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>


