<!-- 5. INGREDIENTS -->
<section class="lsv2-ingredients" id="lsv2-ingredients">
  <div class="lsv2-ingredients-head lsv2-reveal">
    <div class="lsv2-eyebrow">Ingredient dossier</div>
    <h2 class="lsv2-h-section">Three plants. <span class="lsv2-italic">All studied.</span></h2>
    <p class="lsv2-lede">Each oil was selected because it has independently published research on inhalation and craving — not because of how it tastes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lsv2-ingredients-track-wrap">
    <div class="lsv2-ingredients-track" id="lsv2-ing-track">

      <article class="lsv2-ing-card">
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-num">01 · 03</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-latin">Mentha × piperita</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-name">Peppermint</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-chips">
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Cooling</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Menthol</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Throat sensation</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Alertness</span>
        </div>
        <p class="lsv2-ing-card-finding">
          The most-studied aroma in the blend. <strong>Hirsch (2008)</strong> at the Smell &amp; Taste Treatment Foundation found peppermint inhalation reduced caloric intake and lowered hunger ratings. <strong>Kennedy et al. (2018)</strong> in <em>Nutrients</em> reported improved cognition and reduced mental fatigue. The cooling sensation closely mimics the menthol throat-hit familiar to former vape users.
        </p>
        <ul class="lsv2-ing-card-cites">
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="7">7</button> Hirsch, A. R. (2008). <em>Effects of peppermint scent on appetite control.</em></li>
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="8">8</button> Kennedy, D. O., et al. (2018). <em>Volatile terpenes &amp; brain function.</em> Nutrients, 10(8).</li>
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="9">9</button> Kazemi, A., et al. (2024). <em>Peppermint &amp; menthol: review.</em></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="lsv2-ing-card">
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-num">02 · 03</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-latin">Piper nigrum</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-name">Black Pepper</div>
        <div class="lsv2-ing-card-chips">
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Warming</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Earthy</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Throat hit</span>
          <span class="lsv2-ing-card-chip">Most-cited</span>
        </div>
        <p class="lsv2-ing-card-finding">
          The ingredient most directly relevant to Lio's category. <strong>Rose &amp; Behm (1994)</strong> at Duke ran the foundational randomized trial: black-pepper vapor reduced craving and withdrawal versus control in 48 abstinent smokers. <strong>Cordell &amp; Buckle (2013)</strong> extended this on a US campus. <strong>Yılmaz &amp; Karadağ (2022)</strong> confirmed the finding in a randomized pilot.
        </p>
        <ul class="lsv2-ing-card-cites">
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="1">1</button> Rose &amp; Behm (1994). <em>Drug Alcohol Depend.</em>, 34(3), 225–229.</li>
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="5">5</button> Cordell &amp; Buckle (2013). <em>J. Altern. Complement. Med.</em>, 19(8).</li>
          <li><button class="lsv2-ref" data-ref="6">6</button> Yılmaz &amp; Karadağ (2022). RCT pilot on nicotine craving.</li>
